# Semantic pass contracts

This is the reusable semantic-contract layer in v5. It is independent of the CAD, scene and mechanism implementations and independent of the compiler scheduler. The integration adapter connects it to compiler acceptance policies so that both computed and cached artifacts undergo the current contract before publication.

## Definition APIs

All definitions have an explicit `id`, `version` and `description`. Definitions are recursively frozen. A `ContractRegistry` registers exact versions, recursively registers referenced definitions, rejects replacement of an existing version, and exposes serializable manifests through `list()`.

| API | Implemented meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `defineIR<T>` | Identifies an artifact kind/schema version and validates its value. |
| `defineType<T>` | Defines a scalar/JSON port type with a runtime validator. |
| `defineProperty<T>` | Names a proposition about one IR artifact and identifies its checking analysis. |
| `defineAnalysis<T,R>` | Defines a typed, versioned computation on an artifact, with parameters, effect declarations and optional checked transfer rule. |
| `defineInvariant<P>` | Defines a relation over named typed ports, such as an input/output approximation bound. |
| `defineObjective<T>` | Associates a finite numerical analysis with a min/max direction. |
| `defineEffect<T>` | Names read or write access to an exact revisioned resource with a scalar value type. |
| `defineTransformation<I,O>` | Declares named inputs/outputs, preconditions, postconditions, establishment, preservation, correspondence, effects and cache policy. |

`createArtifact(ir,id,value)` validates, copies and freezes the payload and creates the same SHA-256 content/revision identities used by the existing compiler. `validateArtifact` verifies the kind, schema, value validator and both hashes. An artifact cannot pass a contract merely by claiming the expected kind or supplying an old revision next to changed data.

An IR port accepts `Artifact<T>`. A scalar port accepts `T`. `PortValues<P>` provides TypeScript inference across named ports; runtime validation additionally checks exact port names. Included scalar definitions are `Integer`, `FiniteNumber`, `JsonValue` and `Check`.

## Writing a contract

```ts
import * as C from './contracts/index.js';

const MeshIR = C.defineIR<Mesh>({
  id: 'geometry.mesh', version: '1', description: 'Triangle mesh',
  validate: validateMesh
});
const ManifoldCheck = C.defineAnalysis({
  id: 'mesh.manifold-check', version: '1', description: 'Closed topology',
  input: MeshIR, output: C.Check,
  run: (_ctx, artifact) => ({
    ok: meshMetrics(artifact.value).watertight,
    reason: 'Every oriented edge must have its opposite partner'
  })
});
const Manifold = C.defineProperty({
  id: 'mesh.manifold', version: '1', description: 'Closed oriented mesh',
  subject: MeshIR, check: ManifoldCheck
});
const Reduce = C.defineTransformation({
  id: 'mesh.reduce', version: '1', description: 'Checked mesh reduction',
  inputs: { mesh: MeshIR, tolerance: C.FiniteNumber },
  outputs: { mesh: MeshIR },
  requires: [{property: Manifold, subject: {from: 'inputs', port: 'mesh'}}],
  ensures: [{property: Manifold, subject: {from: 'outputs', port: 'mesh'}}],
  cache: 'pure'
});
```

The geometry functions in this sketch are application implementations; the framework does not assume a universal mesh representation. Actual contracts for the included domains live under `src/contract-examples/`.

A condition either checks a property on `{from:'inputs'|'outputs',port}` or binds an invariant's named ports to input/output selectors. The factory checks port compatibility and rejects references to outputs in preconditions. Invariant results must be `{ok:boolean,reason?,evidence?}`; truthy objects or strings are rejected.

The clauses have separate operational meanings:

- `requires` runs before the kernel. Failed requirements prevent execution.
- `ensures` checks output properties or relational invariants before acceptance.
- `establishes` checks and records a newly established output fact.
- `preserves` checks that an input property holds and remains valid for an output. `mode:'check'` runs the target checker; `mode:'transfer'` requires a declared correspondence and an executable authorized transfer rule.
- `correspondence` requires supplied records naming exact input/output artifact revisions and the declared relation.
- `effects` governs access through the revisioned resource port.
- `cache` is `pure`, `tracked` or `never`. Pure definitions declare no resource effects. Writes require `never`.

A preservation declaration is not accepted as its own evidence. Required transfer rules are versioned parts of the analysis definition; redefining their implementation requires a new definition version.

## Analysis manager API

`new AnalysisManager(registry?, resourcePort?)` retains reusable analyses, facts, executions and evidence.

| Method | Result / behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `analyse(definition,artifact,params?)` | Compute or reuse an `AnalysisRecord<R>` for the exact subject, definition version and parameters. |
| `hasAnalysis(definition,artifact,params?)` | Check current reusable availability, including transitive dependencies and explicit invalidations. |
| `check(property,artifact)` | Obtain a checked or otherwise authorized `PropertyFact`; false is a recorded fact, not an exception. |
| `checkInvariant(invariant,namedInputs)` | Query a relational invariant independently; returns checked evidence and records its actual typed inputs. |
| `properties(artifact)` | Inspect current revision-specific facts and their current validity. |
| `explainProperty(property,artifact)` | Explain availability, checker version, assurance, evidence and invalidation for this exact revision. |
| `preserve(analysis,from,to,correspondence,params?)` | Transfer a valid existing result only after the registered transfer checker approves this exact correspondence. |
| `invalidate(analysisOrProperty,artifact,reason?)` | Invalidate information and advance its epoch; dependent information becomes unavailable, including a checker still computing when invalidation occurs. |
| `evaluateObjective(objective,artifact)` | Execute/reuse the numerical analysis and return its value, direction and exact analysis record. |
| `declare(property,artifact,reason)` | Record an unverified declaration. It cannot satisfy a requirement. |
| `recordProof(property,artifact,certificate)` | Invoke the property's explicitly registered external certificate verifier and record its accepted evidence. |
| `snapshot()` | Return immutable manifests, histories, executions and events for inspection. |

Analyses may depend on analyses, properties and versioned resources. These dependencies are retained as exact record IDs and resource revisions. A change to a dependency, definition version, artifact revision or invalidation epoch prevents stale reuse. Cycles across analyses and properties are diagnosed. Analysis/fact identities are deterministic for equivalent computations, so concurrent verification of the same immutable source does not create false conflicts.

Inside an analysis, invariant or transformation, `ContractContext` provides:

```ts
await ctx.analysis(TriangleCount, mesh);       // returns the typed value
await ctx.check(Manifold, mesh);               // returns a fact
await ctx.require(Manifold, mesh);             // throws when not established
await ctx.checkInvariant(WithinBudget, inputs);
ctx.properties(mesh);
ctx.explainProperty(Manifold, mesh);
ctx.readResource(ReadProfile);
ctx.writeResource(WriteExport, value);
ctx.record('measured deviation', evidence);
```

All asynchronous context requests must be awaited. Pending requests are observed, drained and rejected at lifecycle boundaries; they cannot silently finish after output acceptance. Contexts close after their owner returns. Rolling back a transaction immediately closes every descendant context, so pending work cannot publish late analysis or fact records. Every asynchronous query captures artifact revisions, parameters, certificates and correspondences before awaiting caller code. Fact-set/explanation introspection can observe absent information, so its computations and dependants conservatively disable caching rather than pretending an incomplete dependency list captures absence.

## Evidence and transfer

Every property fact identifies its exact artifact ID, revision, kind and schema version, the property and checker versions, the supporting analysis, dependencies, current validity and assurance:

- `declared`: an assertion was recorded, without verification; `ok` is false for authorization purposes.
- `checked`: the specified checker ran or its exact valid evidence was reused.
- `transferred`: an existing valid result was carried to another revision after an authorized transfer checker verified the explicit correspondence.
- `proof`: the property-specific external certificate verifier accepted a supplied certificate.

The last category is an explicit trust boundary. This framework does not contain a theorem prover and does not turn a Boolean checker into a mathematical proof. The record identifies the external verifier and certificate, so callers can evaluate the authority of that evidence. Supplying an assurance string cannot create an accepted fact.

A correspondence contains `id`, exact `from` and `to` artifact references, `relation`, optional entity groups and application evidence. It is small relationship data, not the generic IR editing framework proposed separately. Transfer checks validate exact revisions and their semantic justification. Same-artifact preservation reuses the existing analysis without introducing a self-dependency. Source invalidation also invalidates transferred results.

## Transaction and compiler integration

A transformation can be evaluated directly through `manager.execute(definition,inputs)`. Its optional `run(ctx,inputs)` returns `{outputs,correspondence?}`. The manager validates inputs, executes requirements, computes or reuses the body, checks every guarantee, and then accepts the result.

For an existing compiler, use the explicit transaction lifecycle:

```ts
const tx = await manager.beginTransformation(contract, namedInputs, {
  runId, invocationId
});
// Existing compiler kernel executes; tx.context is its semantic resource/query port.
const prepared = await tx.verify(namedOutputs, correspondences, {cached});
// After every compiler acceptance gate has succeeded:
prepared.commit();
```

`verifyTransformation(before,after,contract,correspondences?,options?)` is a convenience for an already computed lowering. It commits by default; `deferCommit:true` returns a prepared transaction for a host acceptance gate.

`verify` seals the body context immediately, snapshots outputs/correspondences before waiting, and allows only one verification in flight. Output analysis/facts and resource writes remain provisional until synchronous `commit()`. `rollback(reason)` discards them; `fail(error)` additionally retains the original error classification. A closed accepted execution cannot be rewritten as failed. `tx.settle()` drains started semantic work before an asynchronous host failure hook finalizes the error.

Failed provisional analysis/fact records remain in history with invalidation explaining that they were discarded. They are not exposed as current facts and cannot authorize reuse. The execution still contains the rejected checks, exact revisions, effects and error, so failure is inspectable rather than disappearing.

### Computational reuse versus guarantee checks

A cached artifact is still checked under the current contract. The body has its own tracked dependency frame; pre/post checks do not masquerade as computational dependencies.

`execution.bodyDependencies` and `bodyCacheable` describe what the previous kernel used. Before an external compiler reuses that kernel's artifact, it calls `manager.canReuseDependencies(previous.bodyDependencies)`. If reuse is allowed, `tx.importBodyDependencies(...)` validates and imports those dependencies, resource compare-and-swap stamps, permissions and evidence links. Then current guarantees run normally. Importing the complete old manifest on every warm run prevents a second cache hit from dropping body-only dependencies.

The v5 compiler adapter uses its generic pass policy hooks for this sequence. New artifacts are checked before the artifact store and computational cache accept them; cache hits are gated too. A rejected current guarantee cannot expose an old cached artifact as an accepted result of the new run.

## Revisioned effects

`ResourcePort` exposes synchronous snapshot reads and an atomic synchronous commit of staged JSON writes against expected original revisions. `MemoryResources` is the included reference adapter. The port contract requires all-or-nothing commit: it validates all names/values and expected revisions before applying any write. Async external systems can be adapted by preparing a stable snapshot and using an appropriately transactional host commit boundary; they must not claim atomicity they cannot provide. Native async adapter methods and thenable commit results are rejected; rejected asynchronous adapter work is observed but cannot be undone by the manager. The host adapter remains responsible for its atomicity guarantee.

Resource access is checked transitively. An analysis's declared read does not grant its caller permission; the enclosing transformation must declare the effect too. Cached analysis reuse checks the same permissions and dependency revisions. Missing-resource reads record a `missing` revision before throwing, so a caught fallback is invalidated if the resource later appears.

Writes are staged and visible to subsequent reads in the same transaction. Preconditions can read the original resource state, while postconditions see staged revisions. Commit checks the physical original revisions for conflicts and validates post-write dependencies against the proposed final view before installing writes and facts. A rejected postcondition publishes neither the proposed resource state nor its facts.

These rules enforce use of the explicit effect port. They do not sandbox arbitrary JavaScript: an application implementation that directly changes global variables, files or network state outside the port violates the host contract and cannot be made pure by declaration. `cache:'pure'` is not a security boundary. The same qualification applies to caller-supplied validators and transfer/certificate verifiers; their versions and behavior remain an application responsibility.

## Scope

This module implements semantic pass contracts and analysis management. It does not introduce a universal AST, generic rewrite matcher, transaction-based IR editor, e-graph, static effect inference, persistent cache backend or formal proof engine. Those are separate architectural layers.
# Generic IR manipulation

This package implements the third layer independently of the scheduler, semantic contracts and domain implementations. Public exports are in `index.ts`. Domain adapters retain their native representations and expose a finite logical projection for shared algorithms.

## Adapt and open a representation

`defineAdapter(definition)` captures versioned adapter metadata and callbacks from a plain record with own callback properties. Wrap class methods explicitly when adapting an existing class implementation. The mandatory methods are `entities`, `resolve`, `relations` and `describe`; `relationKinds` assigns each named edge kind to containment, dependency, binding, adjacency or provenance. Enumeration and resolution must agree. Duplicate IDs, invalid edge categories, missing endpoints and malformed roots are rejected.

Optional `validate`, `rebuild`, `operation`, `serialize`/`deserialize` and `visualize` functions supply additional capabilities. A rewritable adapter requires a native validator. A serializer and deserializer must be supplied together. Adapter callbacks are synchronous; enumeration may use synchronous iterators. Registration is versioned through `AdapterRegistry`, which rejects changing a registered definition under the same version tuple.

`createIRArtifact(adapter,id,value)` captures finite immutable data and computes its content/revision hashes. `openIR(artifact,adapter,store?)` validates those hashes and binds a view to an exact revision. The optional `IRStore` retains history and owns a compare-and-swap head for each artifact ID. Reopening an existing revision does not advance its head; new revisions are published by edit transactions.

Every reference is `{artifact,revision,entity}`. Methods accept local entity IDs as a convenience, but reject a qualified reference from another artifact or revision. `view.at(committedArtifact)` creates a new view using the same adapter and store. The previous view remains immutable and readable.

## Navigation

`entities`, `resolve`, `describe`, `relations`, `operation`, `find`, `graph`, `serialize` and `visualize` inspect the bound representation. `children`, `parents` and `ancestors` follow containment only. `successors` and `predecessors` follow explicitly named edge kinds. `dependencies` defaults to transitive dependency and binding edges; use `{transitive:false}` for one step.

`walk` supports pre-order, post-order and breadth-first order, selected roots and relation kinds, direction, maximum nodes and maximum depth. It is deterministic and cycle-safe. With no explicit roots it includes disconnected components. Budgets throw `IRBudgetError` instead of returning an incomplete result as success. Default node/depth bounds are 10,000.

`subgraph(selection)` returns the induced logical subgraph. Select `closure:'dependencies'` or `closure:'connected'` for a closure, and optionally restrict its relation kinds and node budget. This is a view projection, not automatically a valid standalone native artifact.

## Transaction lifecycle

`view.beginEdit({label?,verify?})` returns a draft tied to the exact current head and its generation. Available mutations are:

| Method | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `insert(entity)` | Insert a unique stable ID and return a transaction-local handle |
| `replace(target,entity)` | Replace kind/attributes while preserving the entity ID |
| `update(target,attributes)` | Shallow merge attributes |
| `remove(target)` | Remove the entity and its incident relations, without cascading other entities |
| `connect(from,spec,to)` | Add a declared typed relation; supply a stable ID when required by native reconstruction |
| `disconnect(id)` or `disconnect(from,kind,to)` | Remove an exact edge or matching endpoint edges |
| `redirectReferences(from,to,options?)` | Redirect incoming binding/dependency edges by default; other kinds/directions must be explicit |
| `correspond(from,to,relation?)` | Record one-to-one, split, merge, insertion or removal correspondence |

An inserted handle carries the base revision and is valid only inside that draft. It cannot be resolved through the immutable base view. After commit, obtain a reference from the returned revision's view.

`preview()` returns a frozen logical draft. `prepare()` seals mutations, validates the graph, rebuilds native storage, validates its native schema and reprojects the result. It rejects any silent loss or invention of edited logical entities/relations. Native roots are derived by the adapter from the rebuilt value. It then computes actual entity, relation and root changes plus a total correspondence, including implicit retention/addition/removal entries. Preparation does not publish.

`await commit()` optionally verifies the prepared candidate, rechecks the exact head and generation, synchronously accepts semantic state, and publishes the new native revision. The verifier returns `{commit(),rollback()}`; both callbacks must be synchronous. The existing semantic manager's prepared transformation implements that protocol. A rejected candidate never advances the head.

The generation check detects concurrent edits, no-op intervening commits, and an A→B→A content cycle. Native and semantic checks may await before publication, but the final acceptance/publication boundary does not await. Reentrant publication is forbidden. Cancellation through `rollback()` during verification discards subsequent acceptance. A committed draft cannot be reused or rolled back as an undo operation.

Each edit transaction is atomic. A sequence of several committed edits is not automatically one atomic transaction. The rewrite scheduler keeps earlier accepted revisions if a later candidate fails. An enclosing compiler pass has its own publication boundary.

## Differences and correspondence

`diff(beforeView,afterView)` compares logical entities, edge data and roots, including views with different adapters. `affectedEntities(changes)` returns revision-qualified references to changed entities, edge endpoints and changed roots. The edit operation log describes what was requested; the structural difference describes what actually changed.

`makeCorrespondence`, `validateCorrespondence`, `composeCorrespondence`, `invertCorrespondence` and `traceEntity` work independently of a particular native format. A grouped correspondence denotes the Cartesian relation between its source and target IDs. Empty endpoints represent additions or removals. Source and target universes are explicit and fully accounted for.

Composition requires the same intermediate artifact ID, revision and entity universe. Splits and merges compose relationally; creation followed by deletion leaves no surviving endpoint. Inversion reverses historical relations, not the executable edit. Tracing follows an ordered exact-revision chain and retains per-step branching/removal evidence. Default composition and trace bounds are 100,000 pairs/references.

Correspondence records association, not semantic equivalence. A domain checker must justify any preservation or analysis transfer that uses it.

## Patterns and rewriting

See [Patterns and rewrite schedules](PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md) for all builders, matching evidence, registries, seven schedules, order selection, budgets, scoped edit authority and failure statuses. Pure pattern/guard callbacks receive a frozen `IRReader`; rewrite callbacks receive a mutation-only `RewriteEdit`. The scheduler retains prepare/commit authority and seals callback access before semantic acceptance.

## Extension and trust boundaries

The package imposes a common logical inspection/edit protocol, not a physical storage model or universal semantic type system. Adapter callbacks, registered predicates, rewrite implementations and semantic verifiers are trusted TypeScript code. Read-only/mutation-only facades constrain the capabilities passed to callbacks; they are not a JavaScript sandbox against code holding unrelated references.

The revision store is in memory. Traces can be exported, but process recovery, distributed transactions, graph indexes, unbounded streaming and persistent cache services are separate concerns. There is no e-graph engine, SSA package, formal prover or automatic derivation of domain invariants.
# Strategy IR v1

This module is the domain-independent orchestration language in workbench v4. It is a complete implementation of the thirteen operators enumerated in the design. The executable plan is immutable, versioned JSON; functions remain in an explicit algorithm registry. The existing compiler remains responsible for task dependencies, output validation, artifact caching and provenance.

## Public API

Import the namespace to avoid confusing `require` or `parallel` with other APIs:

```ts
import * as S from './strategy/index.js';

const registry = new S.AlgorithmRegistry()
  .register({
    id: 'mesh.reduce', version: '1', kind: 'transform',
    description: 'Reduce a mesh with a recorded error bound',
    run: async (ctx, mesh, args) => {
      // An adapter may require an existing compiler task here.
      // ctx.link({taskId, invocationId, cache, artifact}) records that link.
      return reduceMesh(mesh, args);
    }
  });

const plan = S.plan('mesh-plan', S.sequence('main',
  S.apply('reduce', S.algorithm('mesh.reduce', '1'), {
    args: { tolerance: 0.01 }, bind: 'reduced'
  }),
  S.checkpoint('inspect', 'Reduced result', S.binding('reduced'))
));

const execution = await S.execute(plan, registry, mesh, {
  id: 'compilation-1', maxSteps: 10000, maxConcurrency: 4
});
if (execution.status !== 'succeeded') {
  console.error(execution.error, execution.records);
}
```

The example's `reduceMesh` is an application implementation, not an engine builtin. Real domain adapters and plans are in `src/strategy-examples/`.

### Algorithm registry

`new AlgorithmRegistry().register(algorithm)` registers an exact `(id, version)` pair. Registration cannot replace that pair. `get(ref, expectedKind?)`, `has(ref, expectedKind?)`, `list()` and `snapshot()` provide inspection and exact resolution. A plan is checked against all referenced algorithm versions, including unselected branches, before execution. `plan(id, root, description?, requirements?)` may additionally pin transitive host dependencies in its `requirements` manifest. The compiler examples populate this manifest from the complete task graph, so removing a direct `apply` node cannot silently upgrade a pass still required by another task. Intentional algorithm upgrades require updating the explicit version pins.

An algorithm has `id`, `version`, `kind`, `description` and `run(context, input, args)`. Its kind is one of `transform`, `analysis`, `predicate`, `selector`, `invariant` or `metrics`. Inputs, arguments and returned values must be finite, acyclic JSON data. Outputs are copied and frozen. Undefined, functions, nonfinite numbers, classes, accessors and cycles are rejected.

Algorithm context exposes `signal`, `executionId`, `nodeId`, `path`, `link(link)`, `record(label, data)` and `reject(code, message, evidence?)`. It closes when the algorithm returns. A late write throws without mutating the trace. Links identify actual compiler tasks and invocation IDs; cached invocation details can be attached rather than inferred from a plan.

### Values and lexical references

Arguments and explicit inputs are JSON templates. Ordinary objects/arrays are recursively copied. `ref('current', path?)` reads the current flow value; `ref('input', path?)` reads the initial input to the entire execution. `binding(name, path?)` reads a preceding binding. `ref('item')`, `ref('index')` and `ref('iteration')` are available only in their corresponding bodies. Paths use own object keys or array indices. Prototype path segments are forbidden.

`literal(value)` escapes data containing the reserved `$ref` or `$literal` keys. Plans contain no callback expressions. Predicates, selectors, metric computations and custom convergence identities are named versioned algorithms.

Every node may have `bind` and `label`. A binding publishes that node's result in its containing sequence. A sequence's local bindings are available to following steps in that sequence. Child branches, scopes, loop iterations and nested sequences have isolated local frames. To export a nested computation, bind its result on the composite node. `require` additionally creates its named analysis binding while preserving the current flow value.

### Operators

| Builder | Semantics |
| --- | --- |
| `apply(id, algorithm, options?)` | Run a transform on current or explicit `input`, with templated `args`; return its value. |
| `sequence(id, ...steps)` | Feed each result to the next step; execute an empty sequence as identity. |
| `parallel(id, steps, options?)` | Run independent branches from the same immutable input and isolated bindings; return ordered result array. |
| `fork(id, branches, options?)` | Run named alternatives from the same input; retain successful values and explicit rejected candidates in a `strategy.candidates` value. |
| `chooseBest(id, candidates, objective, options?)` | Execute its candidate-producing child; score every successful candidate, filter metric constraints and return the winning value. |
| `conditional(id, predicate, then, otherwise, options?)` | Require a boolean predicate; execute the selected branch and record the other as skipped. |
| `repeatUntilStable(id, body, options)` | Run the body repeatedly, carrying its result forward; stop only on identity equality. Bound is mandatory. Cycles and nonconvergence fail. |
| `forEach(id, body, options)` | Select an ordered item array using `items`, a selector, or current input. Run body on each item with item/index references; return ordered results. `maxItems` is mandatory. |
| `require(id, analysis, as, options?)` | Run an analysis and bind its result under `as`; return current input unchanged. |
| `verify(id, invariant, options?)` | Require boolean or `{ok, reason?, evidence?}`. False creates an explicit recoverable rejection; success preserves current value. |
| `checkpoint(id, name, data?, options?)` | Record data or current value as evidence and preserve current input. |
| `attempt(id, body, fallback, options?)` | Run body; only an explicit rejection matching optional `codes` activates fallback on the original input. |
| `scope(id, name, body, options?)` | Name and isolate a subcomputation; return its value, without leaking its local bindings. |

`chooseBest` options include `metrics`, `args`, `constraints`, `decisionAs`, `bind` and `label`. `decisionAs` publishes the immutable selection evidence in a named lexical binding while the node continues to return the winning value. It must differ from `bind`. Downstream materializers can receive that decision explicitly alongside the selected artifact, keeping attribution correct when several choices inspect the same candidates. Decision data includes the responsible node ID, objective, constraints, winner, alternatives and tie-breaking rule. Execution IDs remain in the trace rather than in compiler cache parameters. The metrics algorithm returns a numeric map. Without it, each candidate value must expose its map as `metrics`. Objective is `{metric, direction:'min'|'max'}`; constraints support `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `==` with finite thresholds. Missing/nonfinite metrics fail instead of quietly rejecting a candidate. Equal objectives use lexicographic candidate-name comparison by JavaScript code-unit order, independent of host locale. Rejected and infeasible alternatives, individual violations and winner are all retained.

The candidate child can be any node returning a `CandidateSet`, not just `fork`. This supports an item loop followed by an application algorithm that names its resulting candidates.

`repeatUntilStable` defaults to canonical finite-JSON identity. Artifact-shaped values with `id`, `revision`, `kind`, `schemaVersion` and `value` compare `(kind, schemaVersion, revision)` and ignore logical names. A custom `identity` analysis can project the application's semantic identity. The identity analysis is versioned and traced. Fixpoints are directed bounded iteration, not an e-graph or a theorem prover.

## Failure, concurrency and budgets

`StrategyRejected` is the only recoverable exception channel. Algorithms may use `ctx.reject`; failed invariants and empty feasible sets use it. Ordinary exceptions, invalid algorithm outputs, unavailable references, cycles and exceeded budgets remain fatal. `attempt` does not hide implementation bugs. When parallel siblings fail differently, a fatal failure takes precedence over rejection; explicit cancellation takes precedence over rejection when the external signal is aborted. Within a failure class, source-order index gives deterministic error selection.

`parallel`, `fork` and `forEach` support optional local `concurrency`. `ExecuteOptions.maxConcurrency` limits active algorithm calls globally, including nested groups. Only algorithm calls acquire a permit, so composites never hold a permit while waiting for child work. This is asynchronous scheduling, not CPU worker parallelism.

All started operations are awaited after failure. Unscheduled siblings are marked skipped while the trace budget permits. There is no rollback: successful compiler tasks can remain cached even if a later alternative rejects or execution fails. That retained work remains observable.

`maxSteps` counts every allocated node visit, including skipped subtree records, and therefore bounds trace-record allocation. A limit failure does not allocate records for remaining nodes; the nearest recorded ancestor explains the budget failure. `maxDepth` bounds execution nesting; plans are also limited to 10,000 static nodes and static depth 128. `maxItems` and `maxIterations` are required at the corresponding nodes. These are structural budgets, not memory/process isolation or wall-clock preemption. Algorithm evidence payload sizes remain the host's responsibility.

Cancellation is cooperative. The interpreter checks `AbortSignal` between nodes and before/after algorithms and cancels queued semaphore requests. Long asynchronous algorithms must honor `ctx.signal`; synchronous JavaScript cannot be preempted by this interpreter. No fallback catches cancellation. Already-started host work is awaited, not abandoned with an unhandled rejection.

## Validation, editing and interchange

- `validate(value, registry?)` returns `{valid, issues}` with paths and codes. It checks JSON shape, format version, exact fields, IDs, references, lexical binding availability, algorithm categories/versions and numeric bounds.
- `assertValid(value, registry?)` throws `PlanValidationError` with the same issues.
- `walk(plan, visitor?)` returns ordered `{node,parent?,path,depth,slot?}` visits and optionally calls `visitor(node, visit)` in preorder; `find(plan,id)` or `find(plan,predicate)` returns the first matching node or undefined (`predicate(node, visit)`); `childNodes(node)` exposes direct structural children.
- `replace(plan,id,node)`, `insertBefore(plan,id,node)`, `insertAfter(plan,id,node)` and `remove(plan,id)` return new immutable validated plans. Invalid operations leave the old plan untouched. Insertions create a fresh sequence when the target has no sequence parent. Removal supports list entries; a required child or root must be replaced explicitly (an empty sequence is identity).
- `diff(before,after)` reports added, removed, changed and moved nodes by stable node ID. Composite changes refer to child IDs rather than duplicating entire descendant trees. Plan metadata changes use the `$plan` pseudo-ID.
- `serialize(plan, pretty=true)` uses deterministic key ordering; `deserialize(text, registry?)` validates and freezes the JSON interchange value. The workbench has its own lossless YAML view/editor; persisted plan bytes remain normal data.

## Execution evidence

`execute(plan, registry, input, options?)` preflights the plan, snapshots it and the registry, then returns a `StrategyExecution` for succeeded, rejected, failed or cancelled execution. Invalid plans fail preflight with `PlanValidationError` and do not run any algorithm. `executeOrThrow` wraps a nonsuccessful execution in `StrategyExecutionError`, retaining the complete `execution` property.

The execution contains the exact immutable plan, SHA-256 plan revision, algorithm manifest, input, terminal result/error, node records and an event stream. Each dynamic record has its own execution ID, stable plan node ID, parent execution ID, deterministic structural path, iteration/item/branch location, input/output, status, child IDs, evidence, timestamps and compiler invocation/cache links. A repeated or mapped static node has multiple dynamic records; inactive paths are explicitly skipped. Selection, predicates, fixpoint comparisons, checks and recovery decisions are recorded at the responsible nodes.

Pass an explicit execution ID to correlate compiler runs. Otherwise, IDs combine the plan revision and a process-local run counter. Timings describe elapsed inclusive wall time, not exclusive CPU profiling.

`onEvent` receives frozen events. If an observer throws, its error is recorded as `observer-error` evidence and the observer is disabled; compiler semantics remain unchanged. Returned traces are immutable and retain this diagnostic.

## Scope boundary

This module does not implement the separately proposed semantic property/contract system or generic IR rewriting framework. It invokes existing transformations through a clean registry, and composes, edits, validates and explains their strategies. Domain algorithm correctness and semantic equivalence remain the domain compiler's responsibility.

 succeeded in 526ms:
/**
 * The built-in optimization passes over `typed.blocks`, defined with the framework's `definePass` (E3).
 *
 * Each pass body is Compiler Workbench's (passes.js), run once per function through a `Tx` on a working copy of
 * the input revision. The framework computes the declared analyses for the exact input, diffs the result against
 * the input, enforces the declared entity kinds, checks the contract the host supplies (normally
 * `optimizationContract`: the program still means what r0 meant), and checks each preservation claim before the
 * new revision is published. Correspondence between the revisions comes from the origin records the transaction
 * wrote and from the `because` of each removal, so `why` can walk any entity back to the front end.
 */
import { defineTransformation } from '../framework/contracts/definitions.js';
import type { TransformationDefinition } from '../framework/contracts/types.js';
import type { CorrespondenceEntry } from '../framework/ir/types.js';
import { definePass, type Pass } from '../framework/passes/define-pass.js';
import type { DataflowResult } from '../framework/dataflow/analysis.js';
import {
  ANALYSES,
  domView,
  hasEffect,
  isPure,
  liveView,
  rangeView,
  reachView,
  readsOf,
  stmtValues,
  type Dominators,
  type DomView,
  type LiveView,
  type Loop,
  type RangeView,
  type ReachView,
} from './analyses.js';
import { F32_OPERANDS, F32_RESULT } from './f32.js';
import { BlocksIR, blocksAdapter, blocksEntities, entityIdSet } from './ir.js';
import { hash, meaningKept, type MeaningParams } from './evaluate.js';
import { tableBody } from './rules/compile.js';
import { FOLD_RULES } from './rules/fold.js';
import { SIMPLIFY_RULES } from './rules/simplify.js';
import {
  Store,
  typeClass,
  type BlockRec,
  type StmtRec,
  type TypedBlocks,
  type ValueRec,
  type WorkFn,
} from './model.js';
import { Tx, TxError, type Edit, type ValueHandle, type WriteKind } from './tx.js';
import { rotateSpec } from './rotate.js';
import { makeInline } from './inline.js';

type AnalysisName = keyof typeof ANALYSES;
interface FactViews {
  cfg?: unknown;
  dom?: DomView;
  loops?: readonly Loop[];
  live?: LiveView;
  reach?: ReachView;
  ranges?: RangeView;
  [alias: string]: unknown;
}
interface BodyContext {
  readonly st: Store;
  readonly f: WorkFn;
  readonly tx: Tx;
  readonly facts: FactViews;
  readonly params: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
}

export interface TypedPassSpec {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly about: string;
  readonly needs?: Readonly<Record<string, AnalysisName>>;
  readonly writes: readonly WriteKind[];
  readonly preserves?: readonly AnalysisName[];
  body(ctx: BodyContext): void;
  /** A pass over the whole program (it removes functions), not per function: never restricted to one function. */
  readonly program?: true;
}

/** Compiler Workbench write kinds -> the entity and relation kinds of the projection they may change. */
const ENTITY_WRITES: Record<WriteKind, { entities: string[]; relations: string[] }> = {
  values: { entities: ['value.*'], relations: ['operand', 'reads', 'uses'] },
  stmts: {
    entities: ['stmt.*', 'value.*'],
    relations: ['contains', 'uses', 'operand', 'reads', 'writes', 'jump'],
  },
  locals: { entities: ['local'], relations: ['declares'] },
  cfg: {
    entities: ['block', 'stmt.*', 'value.*'],
    relations: ['succ', 'jump', 'contains', 'uses', 'operand', 'reads'],
  },
};

export interface RevisionReport {
  readonly pass: string;
  readonly rev: number;
  readonly edits: readonly Edit[];
  readonly fns: readonly string[];
  readonly kinds: Readonly<Record<string, number>>;
}

const view = (name: AnalysisName, value: unknown, st: Store, fname: string): unknown => {
  switch (name) {
    case 'liveness':
      return liveView(value as DataflowResult, st, fname);
    case 'reaching':
      return reachView(value as DataflowResult, st, fname);
    case 'ranges':
      return rangeView(value as DataflowResult, st, fname);
    case 'dominators':
      return domView((value as Record<string, Dominators>)[fname]);
    default:
      return (value as Record<string, unknown>)[fname];
  }
};

/** Correspondence entries from origins of new entities and the reasons of removals. */
export function revisionCorrespondence(
  before: TypedBlocks,
  after: TypedBlocks,
  edits: readonly Edit[],
): CorrespondenceEntry[] {
  const src = entityIdSet(before),
    dst = entityIdSet(after);
  const groups = new Map<
    string,
    { to: Set<string>; because: Set<string>; notes: string[]; relation: string }
  >();
  const group = (from: string, relation: string) => {
    let g = groups.get(from);
    if (!g)
      groups.set(
        from,
        (g = { to: new Set(dst.has(from) ? [from] : []), because: new Set(), notes: [], relation }),
      );
    return g;
  };
  for (const table of [after.values, after.stmts, after.blocks] as const)
    for (const e of Object.values(table) as (ValueRec | StmtRec | BlockRec)[]) {
      if (src.has(e.id)) continue;
      const o = e.origin[0];
      if (!o || !o.from || !src.has(o.from)) continue;
      const g = group(o.from, o.via);
      g.to.add(e.id);
      for (const b of o.because ?? []) g.because.add(b);
      if (o.note) g.notes.push(o.note);
    }
  for (const e of edits)
    if (e.kind === 'remove' && src.has(e.stmt as string) && !dst.has(e.stmt as string)) {
      const g = group(e.stmt as string, e.pass);
      for (const b of (e.because as string[]) ?? []) g.because.add(b);
      if (e.note) g.notes.push(e.note as string);
    }
  return [...groups].map(([from, g]) => ({
    from: [from],
    to: [...g.to].sort(),
    relation: g.to.size ? g.relation : 'removed',
    ...(g.because.size ? { because: [...g.because].sort() } : {}),
    ...(g.notes.length ? { note: [...new Set(g.notes)].join(' > ') } : {}),
  }));
}

export type TypedPass = Pass<TypedBlocks, Record<string, any>, Record<string, unknown>> & {
  readonly typed: TypedPassSpec;
};

export function typedPass(spec: TypedPassSpec): TypedPass {
  const needs = Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(spec.needs ?? {}).map(([alias, name]) => [alias, ANALYSES[name]]),
  );
  const writes = { entities: new Set<string>(), relations: new Set<string>() };
  for (const k of spec.writes) {
    ENTITY_WRITES[k].entities.forEach(e => writes.entities.add(e));
    ENTITY_WRITES[k].relations.forEach(r => writes.relations.add(r));
  }
  const pass = definePass<TypedBlocks, Record<string, any>, Record<string, unknown>>({
    id: `typed.${spec.name}`,
    version: '1',
    description: spec.about,
    ir: BlocksIR,
    adapter: blocksAdapter,
    needs,
    writes: { entities: [...writes.entities], relations: [...writes.relations] },
    preserves: (spec.preserves ?? []).map(n => ANALYSES[n]),
    run({ before, facts, params }) {
      const st = Store.from(before.value),
        rev = before.value.rev + 1;
      const edits: Edit[] = [],
        touched: string[] = [];
      for (const f of st.fns.values()) {
        const views: FactViews = {};
        for (const [alias, name] of Object.entries(spec.needs ?? {}))
          views[alias] = view(name, facts[alias], st, f.name);
        const tx = new Tx(st, f, { name: spec.name, writes: spec.writes }, rev);
        spec.body({ st, f, tx, facts: views, params: (params ?? {}) as Record<string, unknown> });
        tx.commit();
        if (tx.edits.length) {
          edits.push(...tx.edits);
          touched.push(f.name);
        }
      }
      if (!edits.length) return;
      st.rev = rev;
      const native = st.toNative();
      const kinds = edits.reduce<Record<string, number>>(
        (m, e) => ((m[e.kind] = (m[e.kind] || 0) + 1), m),
        {},
      );
      const report: RevisionReport = { pass: spec.name, rev, edits, fns: touched, kinds };
      return {
        native,
        correspondence: revisionCorrespondence(before.value, native, edits),
        edits: edits.length,
        report,
      };
    },
  });
  return Object.freeze({ ...pass, typed: spec }) as TypedPass;
}

const contracts = new Map<string, TransformationDefinition<any, any>>();
/** The gate every optimization passes: the output still agrees with the origin (r0) on generated cases. */
export function optimizationContract(params: MeaningParams = {}): TransformationDefinition<any, any> {
  const check = meaningKept(params);
  let c = contracts.get(check.version);
  if (!c) {
    c = defineTransformation({
      id: 'typed.optimization',
      version: check.version,
      description: 'an optimization keeps the meaning of the original program',
      inputs: { input: BlocksIR, origin: BlocksIR },
      outputs: { output: BlocksIR },
      ensures: [
        {
          invariant: check,
          bindings: {
            origin: { from: 'inputs', port: 'origin' },
            candidate: { from: 'outputs', port: 'output' },
          },
        },
      ],
    });
    contracts.set(check.version, c);
  }
  return c;
}

// ---------- the passes ----------
const isPow2 = (n: number) => n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) === 0;
const log2 = (n: number) => 31 - Math.clz32(n);
const lastStmt = (st: Store, b: BlockRec) =>
  b.stmts.length ? (st.get(b.stmts.at(-1)!) as StmtRec) : undefined;

/**
 * Folding is i32 arithmetic (Compiler Workbench's rules), plus the f32 operators in `F32_FOLDABLE`, evaluated on
 * bits exactly as the target computes them (f32.ts): rounded to nearest even, which is the rule the target applies
 * statically, so a folded constant is the value the program would compute. Operators that round in another direction
 * (floor, ceil, trunc, the saturating conversions) are not folded.
 *
 * The cases are the rule table FOLD_RULES (rules/fold.ts), one rule per operator, compiled into the rewrite visitor
 * (IRWB-006). The hand-written body it replaced is kept in rules/handwritten.ts; on the corpus (every pipeline) and
 * on Candela's O2 both give identical revisions, edit lists and correspondences.
 */
export const fold = typedPass({
  name: 'fold',
  about: 'constant folding: an operator whose operands are constants becomes a constant',
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops', 'liveness', 'reaching'],
  body: tableBody(FOLD_RULES.rules),
});

/**
 * The identities are i32 identities. None holds for f32 (x + 0 is not x for x = -0; x * 0 is not 0 for NaN or ∞;
 * x - x is not 0 for ∞; !(a < b) is not a >= b for NaN), so f32 operators are left alone; their own names keep
 * them out of the rules.
 *
 * The identities are the rule table SIMPLIFY_RULES (rules/simplify.ts), each rule verified once for all inputs
 * (rules/verify.ts, rules/evidence.ts), compiled into the rewrite visitor (IRWB-006). The hand-written body it
 * replaced is kept in rules/handwritten.ts; both give identical revisions on the corpus and on Candela's O2.
 */
export const simplify = typedPass({
  name: 'simplify',
  about: 'algebraic identities: x+0, x*1, x*2^k, x-x, !!x and inverted comparisons',
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops'],
  body: tableBody(SIMPLIFY_RULES.rules),
});

export const constprop = typedPass({
  name: 'constprop',
  about:
    'constant propagation: a read whose reaching definitions all assign the same constant becomes that constant',
  needs: { reach: 'reaching' },
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops'],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    const reach = facts.reach!;
    for (const b of f.blocks)
      tx.rewrite(
        v => {
          if (v.op !== 'get') return;
          const sid = v.statement;
          if (!sid) return;
          const defs = reach.reachingAt(sid, v.imm as number);
          if (!defs.length) return;
          const consts = defs.map(d =>
            d.startsWith('init:')
              ? 0
              : d.startsWith('param:')
                ? null
                : (st.get((st.get(d) as StmtRec).value!) as ValueRec).op === 'const'
                  ? ((st.get((st.get(d) as StmtRec).value!) as ValueRec).imm as number)
                  : null,
          );
          if (consts.some(c => c === null) || new Set(consts).size !== 1) return;
          const real = defs.filter(d => !d.startsWith('init:'));
          // the constant has the local's type: an f32 local's constant is its bits (+0 for a local's initial 0)
          return tx.const(consts[0]!, {
            because: real.length ? real : [`local:${f.name}/${f.locals[v.imm as number].name} starts at 0`],
            type: typeClass(f.locals[v.imm as number].type),
          });
        },
        [b],
      );
  },
});

export const dce = typedPass({
  name: 'dce',
  about: 'dead-code elimination: an assignment nobody reads, or a value computed for nothing, goes',
  needs: { live: 'liveness' },
  writes: ['stmts'],
  // Compiler Workbench also claims 'loops'; false: adding or removing a `set` in a loop changes its assigned locals.
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators'],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    const live = facts.live!;
    for (const sid of f.blocks.flatMap(b => b.stmts)) {
      const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
      if (s.op === 'set' && !live.liveAfter(s.id).has(s.local!) && !hasEffect(st, s.value!))
        tx.remove(s.id, { because: [`fact:liveness(${f.name})@r${tx.rev - 1}`] });
      else if (s.op === 'eval' && !hasEffect(st, s.value!)) tx.remove(s.id, { because: ['no effect'] });
    }
  },
});

export const cfg = typedPass({
  name: 'cfg',
  about: 'control-flow cleanup: constant branches, jumps through empty blocks, merging straight-line blocks',
  needs: { cfg: 'cfg' },
  writes: ['stmts', 'cfg'],
  preserves: [],
  body({ st, f, tx }) {
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      const last = lastStmt(st, b);
      if (!last || last.op !== 'br_if') continue;
      const c = st.get(last.cond!) as ValueRec;
      if (c.op === 'const')
        tx.replaceStmt(
          last.id,
          { op: 'br', to: c.imm ? last.then : last.else },
          { because: [c.id], note: 'branch on a constant' },
        );
      else if (last.then === last.else && isPure(st, last.cond!))
        tx.replaceStmt(
          last.id,
          { op: 'br', to: last.then },
          { because: [last.id], note: 'both arms go to the same block' },
        );
    }
    // thread jumps through blocks that only jump
    const forward = new Map<string, string>();
    for (const b of f.blocks)
      if (
        b.stmts.length === 1 &&
        (st.get(b.stmts[0]) as StmtRec).op === 'br' &&
        b.id !== f.blocks[0].id &&
        (st.get(b.stmts[0]) as StmtRec).to !== b.id
      )
        forward.set(b.id, (st.get(b.stmts[0]) as StmtRec).to!);
    const final = (id: string) => {
      const seen = new Set<string>();
      while (forward.has(id) && !seen.has(id)) {
        seen.add(id);
        id = forward.get(id)!;
      }
      return id;
    };
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      const last = lastStmt(st, b);
      if (!last) continue;
      for (const field of ['to', 'then', 'else'] as const)
        if (last[field] && final(last[field]!) !== last[field])
          tx.setField(last.id, field, final(last[field]!), { because: [last[field]!] });
    }
    tx.commit();
    // merge b into a when a ends with br b and b has a as its only predecessor
    let merged = true;
    while (merged) {
      merged = false;
      const preds = new Map(f.blocks.map(b => [b.id, [] as string[]]));
      for (const b of f.blocks) for (const s of b.succ) if (preds.has(s)) preds.get(s)!.push(b.id);
      for (const a of f.blocks) {
        const last = lastStmt(st, a);
        if (!last || last.op !== 'br') continue;
        const bId = last.to!;
        if (bId === a.id || bId === f.blocks[0].id || preds.get(bId)!.length !== 1) continue;
        const b = st.get(bId) as BlockRec;
        tx.remove(last.id, { because: [bId], note: 'merged with its only successor' });
        for (const sid of b.stmts.slice())
          tx.move(sid, { block: a, index: a.stmts.length }, { because: [last.id] });
        a.succ = b.succ.slice();
        b.succ = [];
        tx.record('merge', { block: bId, into: a.id });
        tx.commit();
        merged = true;
        break;
      }
    }
  },
});

export const licm = typedPass({
  name: 'licm',
  about:
    'loop-invariant code motion: pure expressions whose operands the loop never assigns move to the preheader',
  needs: { loops: 'loops' },
  writes: ['stmts', 'values', 'locals', 'cfg'],
  preserves: [],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    for (const L of facts.loops!) {
      let pre = L.preheader;
      if (!pre) {
        pre = tx.block({ because: [L.id], note: 'preheader' });
        tx.insert(tx.at.start(pre), tx.stmt({ op: 'br', to: L.head }, { because: [L.id] }), {
          because: [L.id],
        });
        for (const b of f.blocks) {
          if (L.body.includes(b.id) || b.id === pre) continue;
          const last = lastStmt(st, b);
          if (!last) continue;
          for (const field of ['to', 'then', 'else'] as const)
            if (last[field] === L.head) tx.setField(last.id, field, pre, { because: [L.id] });
        }
        if (f.blocks[0].id === L.head) throw new TxError('unsupported', 'licm: a loop at the entry block');
      }
      const assigned = new Set(L.assigned);
      const INV = [
        'get',
        'const',
        'add',
        'sub',
        'mul',
        'shl',
        'shr',
        'and',
        'or',
        'xor',
        'eq',
        'ne',
        'lt',
        'gt',
        'le',
        'ge',
        'eqz',
        // f32 operators are pure and never trap, so hoisting one out of a loop is safe
        ...F32_OPERANDS,
        ...F32_RESULT,
        'aaddr',
      ];
      const inv = (id: string): boolean => {
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        if (!INV.includes(v.op)) return false;
        if (v.op === 'get') return !assigned.has(v.imm as number);
        return v.args.every(inv);
      };
      const hoist = (id: string): string => {
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        if (v.op !== 'const' && v.op !== 'get' && inv(id)) {
          const t = tx.local('inv', { because: [L.id], type: v.type ?? 'i32' });
          const set = tx.set(t, id, { because: [L.id], note: `hoisted out of ${L.id}` });
          tx.insert(tx.at.end(pre!), set, { because: [L.id] });
          return tx.get(t, { from: id, because: [set] });
        }
        const args = v.args.map(hoist);
        return args.some((a, i) => a !== v.args[i])
          ? tx.make(v.op, ...args, { imm: v.imm, from: id, type: v.type ?? 'i32' })
          : id;
      };
      for (const bid of L.body)
        for (const sid of (st.get(bid) as BlockRec).stmts.slice()) {
          const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
          for (const k of ['value', 'cond', 'addr'] as const)
            if (s[k]) {
              const n = hoist(s[k]!);
              if (n !== s[k]) tx.setField(sid, k, n, { because: [L.id] });
            }
        }
    }
  },
});

export const lvn = typedPass({
  name: 'lvn',
  about: 'local value numbering: the same pure expression computed twice in a block is computed once',
  writes: ['stmts', 'values', 'locals'],
  // Compiler Workbench also claims 'loops'; false: adding or removing a `set` in a loop changes its assigned locals.
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators'],
  body({ st, f, tx }) {
    // a key names the operator, its immediate (an array offset, a load offset) and type, so an f32 constant and an
    // i32 constant with the same bits, or two frame addresses, never share a number
    const key = (id: string): string => {
      const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
      const t = v.type === 'f32' ? 'f' : '';
      return v.op === 'const'
        ? `#${t}${v.imm}`
        : v.op === 'get'
          ? `@${v.imm}`
          : `${v.op}${v.imm === undefined ? '' : `[${v.imm}]`}${t}(${v.args.map(key).join(',')})`;
    };
    const size = (id: string): number => 1 + (st.get(id) as ValueRec).args.reduce((n, a) => n + size(a), 0);
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      const seen = new Map<string, { stmt: string; value: string }>();
      for (const sid of b.stmts.slice()) {
        const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
        const visit = (id: string): boolean => {
          const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
          if (v.op !== 'tuple' && v.args.length && size(id) >= 3 && isPure(st, id)) {
            const k = key(id),
              first = seen.get(k);
            if (first && first.value !== id) {
              const t = tx.local('vn', { because: [first.value], type: v.type ?? 'i32' });
              const set = tx.set(t, first.value, {
                because: [first.value, id],
                note: `computed once for ${first.stmt} and ${sid}`,
              });
              tx.replace(first.value, tx.get(t, { from: first.value, because: [set] }));
              tx.replace(id, tx.get(t, { from: id, because: [set] }));
              tx.insert(tx.at.before(first.stmt), set, { because: [first.value, id] });
              seen.delete(k);
              return true;
            }
            if (!first) seen.set(k, { stmt: sid, value: id });
          }
          return v.args.some(visit);
        };
        for (const k of ['addr', 'value', 'cond'] as const) if (s[k] && visit(s[k]!)) break;
        if (s.op === 'set')
          for (const [k, e] of [...seen]) if (readsOf(st, e.value).has(s.local!)) seen.delete(k);
      }
    }
  },
});

export const strength = typedPass({
  name: 'strength',
  about:
    'strength reduction with proof: x / 2^k and x % 2^k become shifts and masks when ranges prove x is not negative',
  needs: { ranges: 'ranges' },
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops'],
  body({ f, tx, facts }) {
    const ranges = facts.ranges!;
    tx.rewrite(v => {
      if ((v.op !== 'div' && v.op !== 'rem') || !v.args[1].isConst || !isPow2(v.args[1].imm as number))
        return;
      const sid = v.statement!,
        r = ranges.of(sid, v.args[0].id);
      if (r[0] < 0) return;
      const k = v.args[1].imm as number,
        why = [`fact:ranges(${f.name})@r${tx.rev - 1}: ${v.args[0].text} in [${r[0]}, ${r[1]}]`];
      return v.op === 'div'
        ? tx.make('shr', v.args[0].id, tx.const(log2(k)), { because: why, note: `x / ${k} with x ≥ 0` })
        : tx.make('and', v.args[0].id, tx.const(k - 1), { because: why, note: `x % ${k} with x ≥ 0` });
    });
  },
});

export const divToShift = typedPass({
  name: 'div-to-shift',
  about: 'deliberately wrong: x / 2^k becomes x >> k with no proof that x is not negative',
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops'],
  body: ({ tx }) =>
    tx.rewrite(v => {
      if (v.op === 'div' && v.args[1].isConst && isPow2(v.args[1].imm as number))
        return tx.make('shr', v.args[0].id, tx.const(log2(v.args[1].imm as number)), {
          note: 'no range check: wrong for negative x',
        });
    }),
});

export const bce = typedPass({
  name: 'bce',
  about:
    'bounds-check elimination: a check i >=u buf.length goes when a dominating branch proved i < buf.length and ranges prove i ≥ 0',
  needs: { dom: 'dominators', reach: 'reaching', ranges: 'ranges' },
  writes: ['stmts', 'cfg'],
  preserves: [],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    const dom = facts.dom!,
      reach = facts.reach!,
      ranges = facts.ranges!;
    // resolve a read through single copies: t = x  ->  x (when t has one definition)
    const resolve = (
      sid: string,
      id: string,
    ): { id: string; local: number | null; sid: string; via?: string } => {
      const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
      if (v.op !== 'get') return { id, local: null, sid };
      const defs = reach.reachingAt(sid, v.imm as number);
      if (defs.length === 1 && defs[0].startsWith('s:')) {
        const d = st.get(defs[0]) as StmtRec,
          dv = st.get(d.value!) as ValueRec;
        if (dv.op === 'get') return { id: d.value!, local: dv.imm as number, sid: d.id, via: d.id };
      }
      return { id, local: v.imm as number, sid };
    };
    const isLength = (id: string, buf?: number) => {
      const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
      return (
        v.op === 'load' &&
        v.imm === 0 &&
        (st.get(v.args[0]) as ValueRec).op === 'get' &&
        (buf === undefined || (st.get(v.args[0]) as ValueRec).imm === buf)
      );
    };
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      const last = lastStmt(st, b);
      if (!last || last.op !== 'br_if') continue;
      const c = st.get(last.cond!) as ValueRec;
      if (c.op !== 'geu' || !isLength(c.args[1])) continue;
      const trapB = st.get(last.then!) as BlockRec;
      if (!trapB.stmts.length || (st.get(trapB.stmts[0]) as StmtRec).op !== 'trap') continue;
      const idx = resolve(last.id, c.args[0]),
        buf = (st.get((st.get(c.args[1]) as ValueRec).args[0]) as ValueRec).imm as number;
      if (idx.local === null) continue;
      // walk up the dominator tree for a branch whose taken edge proves idx < buf.length
      for (let d = dom.idom.get(b.id), child = b.id; d && d !== child; child = d, d = dom.idom.get(d)) {
        const t = lastStmt(st, st.get(d) as BlockRec);
        if (!t || t.op !== 'br_if') continue;
        let cond = st.get(t.cond!) as ValueRec,
          onTrue = true;
        if (cond.op === 'eqz') {
          cond = st.get(cond.args[0]) as ValueRec;
          onTrue = false;
        }
        if (cond.op === 'ge')
          onTrue = !onTrue; // x >= len taken means x < len on the other arm
        else if (cond.op !== 'lt') continue;
        const arm = onTrue ? t.then! : t.else!;
        if (!dom.dominates(arm, b.id)) continue;
        const x = st.get(cond.args[0]) as ValueRec;
        if (x.op !== 'get' || x.imm !== idx.local || !isLength(cond.args[1], buf)) continue;
        // the index and the buffer are the same values at the branch and at the check
        const same = (l: number) => {
          const a = reach.reachingAt(t.id, l).sort().join(),
            z = reach.reachingAt(idx.sid, l).sort().join();
          return a === z;
        };
        if (!same(idx.local) || !same(buf)) continue;
        if (ranges.at(t.id, idx.local)[0] < 0) continue;
        tx.replaceStmt(
          last.id,
          { op: 'br', to: last.else },
          {
            because: [t.id, `fact:ranges(${f.name}): ${f.locals[idx.local].name} ≥ 0`],
            note: `${t.id} already proved ${f.locals[idx.local].name} < length`,
          },
        );
        break;
      }
    }
  },
});

/**
 * Bounds-check merging for checked text addresses. `caddr(r, p)` traps unless p <u capacity(r) and gives r + 2p.
 * When one block computes several of them on the same receiver, with positions that are one base plus constant
 * offsets (p = B + k, B possibly 0), checking the smallest and the largest position covers every position between:
 * if B + kmin and B + kmax are both below the capacity, so is every B + k with kmin ≤ k ≤ kmax. (For a symbolic
 * B this uses that a text's capacity is below 2^31 units, as its units occupy 2·capacity bytes of a 32-bit
 * address space, so B + kmin + (kmax - kmin) cannot wrap. For constant positions, all ≥ 0, the largest suffices.)
 *
 * The group's first site checks the largest position and its second site the smallest, each rewritten as
 * `caddr(r, p + Δ) - 2Δ` when its own position is not the one checked; every later site becomes the unchecked
 * `r + 2p`. The merged checks run before the original check of the largest position did, which changes only
 * where the program traps, never whether it does:
 * - every caddr of a block runs whenever the block runs (no value operator evaluates its arguments
 *   conditionally), unless something traps first;
 * - a trap aborts the program.
 * A group ends at anything that could break that or change a capacity: a call (it might not return), a store not
 * provably inside a buffer's data, or `zero`.
 *
 * Linear forms are exact modulo 2^32 (add, sub, shifts and products by constants). A symbolic term names either a
 * definition earlier in the same block (one execution per block execution, so equal names are equal values) or
 * a local the block never assigns.
 */
export const bcm = typedPass({
  name: 'bcm',
  about:
    'bounds-check merging: checked text addresses on one receiver whose positions differ by constants are checked once for the smallest and once for the largest position',
  needs: { reach: 'reaching' },
  writes: ['values'],
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators', 'loops'],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    const reach = facts.reach!;
    type Lin = { t: Map<string, number>; k: number };
    type Site = { vid: string; sid: string; key: string; k: number; symbolic: boolean };
    /** keep: the site's own check is one of the group's; check: rewrite to check `check`; unchecked: r + 2p */
    type Plan = { site: Site; first: Site; mode: 'keep' | 'check' | 'unchecked'; check?: number };
    const plan = new Map<string, Plan>();
    const isLit = (v: ValueRec) => v.op === 'const' && !v.type;
    const add = (a: Lin, b: Lin, sign = 1): Lin => {
      const t = new Map(a.t);
      for (const [x, c] of b.t) t.set(x, ((t.get(x) ?? 0) + sign * c) | 0);
      return { t, k: (a.k + sign * b.k) | 0 };
    };
    const scale = (a: Lin, c: number): Lin => ({
      t: new Map([...a.t].map(([x, y]) => [x, Math.imul(y, c)])),
      k: Math.imul(a.k, c),
    });
    const baseKey = (a: Lin) =>
      [...a.t]
        .filter(([, c]) => c !== 0)
        .sort(([x], [y]) => (x < y ? -1 : x > y ? 1 : 0))
        .map(([x, c]) => `${c}*${x}`)
        .join('+');
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      const index = new Map(b.stmts.map((sid, i) => [sid, i]));
      const assigned = new Set<number>();
      for (const sid of b.stmts) {
        const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
        if (s.op === 'set') assigned.add(s.local!);
      }
      // a local read at `sid` as a stable name within one execution of the block, or null
      const name = (sid: string, l: number): { def?: StmtRec; key: string } | null => {
        const defs = reach.reachingAt(sid, l);
        if (defs.length === 1 && defs[0].startsWith('s:')) {
          const d = st.get(defs[0]) as StmtRec;
          if (index.has(d.id) && index.get(d.id)! < index.get(sid)!) return { def: d, key: d.id };
          // a single definition of a literal is the same constant wherever it reaches
          if (d.op === 'set' && isLit(st.get(d.value!) as ValueRec)) return { def: d, key: `=${d.id}` };
        }
        if (!assigned.has(l)) return { key: `L${l}` };
        return null;
      };
      const lin = (sid: string, id: string, depth = 0): Lin | null => {
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        if (v.type) return null;
        if (isLit(v)) return { t: new Map(), k: v.imm as number };
        if (depth > 12) return null;
        if (v.op === 'get') {
          const n = name(sid, v.imm as number);
          if (!n) return null;
          if (n.def) {
            const inner = lin(n.def.id, n.def.value!, depth + 1);
            if (inner) return inner;
          }
          return { t: new Map([[n.key, 1]]), k: 0 };
        }
        const x = v.args[0] && lin(sid, v.args[0], depth + 1),
          y = v.args[1] && lin(sid, v.args[1], depth + 1);
        if (!x || !y) return null;
        const yc = y.t.size === 0 || [...y.t.values()].every(c => c === 0) ? y.k : null;
        const xc = x.t.size === 0 || [...x.t.values()].every(c => c === 0) ? x.k : null;
        switch (v.op) {
          case 'add':
            return add(x, y);
          case 'sub':
            return add(x, y, -1);
          case 'shl':
            return yc !== null && yc >= 0 && yc < 32 ? scale(x, (2 ** yc) | 0) : null;
          case 'mul':
            return yc !== null ? scale(x, yc) : xc !== null ? scale(y, xc) : null;
          default:
            return null;
        }
      };
      const recvKey = (sid: string, id: string): string | null => {
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        if (v.op !== 'get') return null;
        return name(sid, v.imm as number)?.key ?? null;
      };
      let open = new Map<string, Site[]>();
      const close = () => {
        const role = (x: Site, first: Site, target: number) =>
          plan.set(
            x.vid,
            x.k === target
              ? { site: x, first, mode: 'keep' }
              : { site: x, first, mode: 'check', check: target },
          );
        for (const sites of open.values()) {
          if (sites.length < 2) continue;
          const ks = sites.map(x => x.k),
            max = Math.max(...ks),
            min = Math.min(...ks);
          const [first, second, ...rest] = sites;
          if (!first.symbolic || min === max) {
            // constant positions ≥ 0, or one position repeated: one check covers the group
            role(first, first, max);
            for (const x of [second, ...rest]) plan.set(x.vid, { site: x, first, mode: 'unchecked' });
          } else {
            if (sites.length < 3) continue;
            role(first, first, max);
            role(second, first, min);
            for (const x of rest) plan.set(x.vid, { site: x, first, mode: 'unchecked' });
          }
        }
        open = new Map();
      };
      // a store keeps every capacity word intact when it writes a buffer's data: a checked caddr at a constant
      // position c ≥ 0 with 2c + offset ≥ 4 (the capacity is the first 4 bytes)
      const safeStore = (s: StmtRec): boolean => {
        const a = st.get(s.addr!) as ValueRec;
        if (a.op !== 'caddr') return false;
        const p = lin(s.id, a.args[1]);
        return !!p && baseKey(p) === '' && p.k >= 0 && 2 * p.k + (s.offset ?? 0) >= 4;
      };
      const visit = (sid: string, id: string): void => {
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        v.args.forEach(a => visit(sid, a));
        if (v.op === 'call') close();
        else if (v.op === 'caddr') {
          const recv = recvKey(sid, v.args[0]),
            p = lin(sid, v.args[1]);
          if (recv === null || !p) return;
          const base = baseKey(p);
          if (!base && p.k < 0) return;
          const key = `${recv}|${base}`;
          const sites = open.get(key) ?? [];
          sites.push({ vid: id, sid, key, k: p.k, symbolic: base !== '' });
          open.set(key, sites);
        }
      };
      for (const sid of b.stmts) {
        const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
        // the evaluator's order: a store's address, then its value; otherwise the one value or condition
        const fields = s.op === 'store' ? (['addr', 'value'] as const) : (['value', 'cond'] as const);
        for (const k of fields) if (s[k]) visit(sid, s[k]!);
        if ((s.op === 'store' && !safeStore(s)) || s.op === 'zero') close();
      }
      close();
    }
    if (![...plan.values()].some(p => p.mode !== 'keep')) return;
    tx.rewrite(v => {
      const p = plan.get(v.id);
      if (!p || p.mode === 'keep' || v.op !== 'caddr') return;
      const recv = v.args[0].id,
        pos = v.args[1].id;
      if (p.mode === 'check') {
        const d = (p.check! - p.site.k) | 0;
        const checked = tx.make('caddr', recv, tx.make('add', pos, tx.const(d)), {
          note: `checks position ${p.site.symbolic ? 'base + ' : ''}${p.check} for the group on this receiver`,
        });
        return tx.make('add', checked, tx.const(Math.imul(-2, d)), {
          note: 'this address, from the group check',
        });
      }
      return tx.make('add', recv, tx.make('shl', pos, tx.const(1)), {
        because: [p.first.sid],
        note: `covered by the group's checks, made where ${p.first.sid} computes the first address on this receiver`,
      });
    });
  },
});

/**
 * Dead-function elimination: functions no call reaches from the program's entries are removed. The entries are a
 * parameter (`params.roots`: the functions the host calls, e.g. the four of Candela's gallery slot); a program
 * cannot know them from its own text, since an uncalled function may be exactly what the host calls.
 *
 * Valid under a contract that compares only the entries (`MeaningParams.roots`): a removed function was not
 * callable from any entry, so no entry's result can depend on it. Typically run after a specialization has turned
 * dispatches into direct calls, when whole families of functions lose their last caller.
 */
const dfeDefinition = definePass<TypedBlocks, Record<string, any>, Record<string, unknown>>({
  id: 'typed.dfe',
  version: '1',
  description:
    'dead-function elimination: functions no call reaches from the entries (params.roots) are removed',
  ir: BlocksIR,
  adapter: blocksAdapter,
  needs: {},
  writes: {
    entities: ['fn', 'local', 'block', 'stmt.*', 'value.*', 'op'],
    relations: ['declares', 'contains', 'emits', 'uses', 'operand', 'reads', 'writes', 'succ', 'jump'],
  },
  preserves: [],
  run({ before, params }) {
    const roots = ((params ?? {}) as { roots?: readonly string[] }).roots ?? [];
    if (!roots.length)
      throw new TxError('unsupported', 'dfe needs params.roots: the functions the host calls');
    const st = Store.from(before.value);
    for (const r of roots) st.fn(r);
    const callees = (f: WorkFn): string[] => {
      const out = new Set<string>();
      const walk = (id: string | undefined) => {
        if (!id) return;
        const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
        if (v.op === 'call') out.add(v.imm as string);
        v.args.forEach(walk);
      };
      for (const b of f.blocks)
        for (const sid of b.stmts) {
          const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
          walk(s.value);
          walk(s.cond);
          walk(s.addr);
        }
      return [...out];
    };
    const reached = new Set<string>(roots),
      todo = [...roots];
    while (todo.length)
      for (const c of callees(st.fn(todo.pop()!))) if (!reached.has(c)) (reached.add(c), todo.push(c));
    const dead = [...st.fns.keys()].filter(n => !reached.has(n));
    if (!dead.length) return;
    const rev = before.value.rev + 1;
    for (const n of dead) {
      const f = st.fn(n);
      st.fns.delete(n);
      st.ents.delete(f.id);
      for (const id of [...st.ents.keys()]) if (id.startsWith(`op:${n}#`)) st.ents.delete(id);
    }
    st.rev = rev;
    const native = st.toNative();
    const because = [`entries: ${[...roots].sort().join(', ')}`];
    const after = new Set(blocksEntities(native).map(e => e.id));
    const correspondence: CorrespondenceEntry[] = blocksEntities(before.value)
      .map(e => e.id)
      .filter(id => !after.has(id))
      .sort()
      .map(id => ({ from: [id], to: [], relation: 'removed', because }));
    const edits: Edit[] = dead.map((fn, i) => ({
      id: `edit:r${rev}.${i}`,
      rev,
      pass: 'dfe',
      fn,
      kind: 'removeFn',
      because,
      note: `no call reaches ${fn} from the entries`,
    }));
    const report: RevisionReport = { pass: 'dfe', rev, edits, fns: dead, kinds: { removeFn: dead.length } };
    return { native, correspondence, edits: edits.length, report };
  },
});
export const dfe: TypedPass = Object.freeze({
  ...dfeDefinition,
  typed: {
    name: 'dfe',
    about: 'dead-function elimination: functions no call reaches from the entries (params.roots) are removed',
    writes: ['cfg', 'locals'],
    program: true,
    body: () => {
      throw new TxError('unsupported', 'dfe works on the whole program');
    },
  },
}) as TypedPass;

const countReads = (st: Store, id: string, l: number): number => {
  const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
  return (v.op === 'get' && v.imm === l ? 1 : 0) + v.args.reduce((n, a) => n + countReads(st, a, l), 0);
};
const copyTree = (tx: Tx, st: Store, id: string, from: string, because: string): string => {
  const v = st.get(id) as ValueRec;
  return tx.make(v.op, ...v.args.map(a => copyTree(tx, st, a, from, because)), {
    imm: v.imm,
    from: id,
    because: [because],
    type: v.type ?? 'i32',
  });
};

export const subst = typedPass({
  name: 'subst',
  about:
    'forward substitution: a temporary assigned a pure value and read once in the same block is replaced by the value',
  needs: { live: 'liveness' },
  writes: ['stmts', 'values'],
  // Compiler Workbench also claims 'loops'; false: adding or removing a `set` in a loop changes its assigned locals.
  preserves: ['cfg', 'dominators'],
  body({ st, f, tx, facts }) {
    const live = facts.live!;
    const defCount = new Map<number, number>();
    for (const b of f.blocks)
      for (const sid of b.stmts) {
        const s = st.get(sid) as StmtRec;
        if (s.op === 'set') defCount.set(s.local!, (defCount.get(s.local!) || 0) + 1);
      }
    for (const b of f.blocks) {
      for (let i = 0; i < b.stmts.length; i++) {
        const d = st.get(b.stmts[i]) as StmtRec;
        if (
          d.op !== 'set' ||
          defCount.get(d.local!) !== 1 ||
          f.locals[d.local!].param ||
          !isPure(st, d.value!)
        )
          continue;
        const operands = readsOf(st, d.value!);
        let users = 0,
          user: StmtRec | null = null,
          blocked = false;
        for (let j = i + 1; j < b.stmts.length; j++) {
          const s = st.get(b.stmts[j]) as StmtRec;
          const n = stmtValues(s).reduce((m, v) => m + countReads(st, v, d.local!), 0);
          if (n) {
            users += n;
            if (!user) user = s;
            else break;
          }
          if (s.op === 'set' && (operands.has(s.local!) || s.local === d.local)) {
            if (!user) blocked = true;
            break;
          }
        }
        if (blocked || users !== 1 || live.liveAfter(user!.id).has(d.local!)) continue;
        tx.rewrite(
          v => (v.op === 'get' && v.imm === d.local ? copyTree(tx, st, d.value!, v.id, d.id) : undefined),
          [b],
        );
        tx.remove(d.id, { because: [user!.id], note: 'substituted into its only reader' });
        break; // one per block per run: liveness changed; the pipeline repeats
      }
    }
  },
});

/** IRWB-003: loop rotation by header duplication (src/typed/rotate.ts). */
export const rotate = typedPass(rotateSpec);
/** IRWB-003: inlining, a whole-program pass like dfe (src/typed/inline.ts). */
export const inline = makeInline(revisionCorrespondence);

export const PASSES: Readonly<Record<string, TypedPass>> = Object.freeze({
  fold,
  simplify,
  constprop,
